SOAPBOX: NCCC wanted to make a club effort and the rotators were
SOAPBOX: just repaired, so it was time for a contest!
SOAPBOX: Only three ops available, and a late start Friday, but we
SOAPBOX: eventually put in 27 hours of operating, and still met our
SOAPBOX: other obligations for the weekend. We were all remote from
SOAPBOX: the mountaintop, and we each had two or three problems with
SOAPBOX: the remote desktop or logging program. So now our hardware
SOAPBOX: works, but the software is acting up. We just operated one
SOAPBOX: operator at a time, with cluster spots, and no extra 2nd ops
SOAPBOX: or spotting radios.
SOAPBOX: High antennas and high power allowed us to work far DX each
SOAPBOX: day, working Europeans, Japan, Indonesia, ... all in the log!
SOAPBOX: We worked 72 DXCC total, but QSOs were highly USA, Japan and
SOAPBOX: Canada. 15m band was the star, but 10m showed up and was no
SOAPBOX: slouch. Rate varied a lot, with hourly rates from 70 down to
SOAPBOX: 15 probably depending on the hourly conditions, band and if
SOAPBOX: we were running or S&P. Whatever the rate, we had fun and
SOAPBOX: switched ops enough to stay alert. A good contest outing,
SOAPBOX: thank you for the contacts!
SOAPBOX: ND2T (NN6U K6TD K6UFO @K6MTU)
SOAPBOX: Flex 6700 and SmartSDR software
SOAPBOX: Writelog, MMTTY and 2Tone software
SOAPBOX: Alpha 9500 amp
SOAPBOX: JK Mid-Tribander at 95 feet high
SOAPBOX: JK 4 el 40m yagi at 110 feet
SOAPBOX: 80m 4-square array
